From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Leave garbage collection out of request CPU, collect idle
 isolates and size the runtime heap

A full collection runs inside the request whose allocation triggered it, and the CPU
limit charged all of it to that request, terminating it and its isolate. Its thread
CPU is now excluded from the request, up to a token bucket per Worker
(COWORKERD_GC_BUDGET_MS, refilled at COWORKERD_GC_REFILL_MS_PER_S) that a new isolate
of the same Worker continues; beyond it a collection is charged as before. A
collection that pauses its process at least COWORKERD_GC_LONG_PAUSE_MS writes a
gc-pause row where the CPU limit writes its rows. The idle reaper collects a dynamic
isolate between requests once it has been idle COWORKERD_IDLE_GC_MS and its old
generation grew COWORKERD_IDLE_GC_MIN_MIB -- never while the event loop has been busy
within that time, which its own late ticks show, and never an isolate hosting actors;
actor events reach the reaper through topUpActor(), which now tops an actor's CPU up
for each new event as the interface prescribes. Static isolates take
COWORKERD_STATIC_HEAP_MIB as their heap. The lifecycle snapshot carries the process's
collection counts.

